<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Verdun</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Verdun is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> since late Roman times. The treaty that divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> was signed there in 843, and its bishops later ruled a principality of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. France occupied Verdun with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> in 1552 and gained it formally in 1648. Fortified by Vauban, it became a great frontier fortress and gave its name to the longest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in 1916.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/verdun.jpg" alt="Verdun" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aimelaime · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porte_Verdun.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Verdun stands on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> in the north of the Meuse department, in Grand Est. It lies 225 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and 184 km from Strasbourg, and is the largest city of the department, although the prefecture is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bar-le-duc">Bar-le-Duc</a>, 47 km to the south. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> founded it, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name, Verodunum, is a latinisation of a Gaulish place name meaning strong fort. In 486 it refused to yield to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> after their victory in the Franco-Roman War and was besieged by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Verdun became a free imperial city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1374, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> it formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a>. A large bastioned citadel was built from 1624 to 1636 on the site of the Abbey of Saint Vanne. In 1670 Sébastien Le Prestre de Vauban visited and drew up a scheme to fortify the whole city; much of it was carried out in the following decades, and some elements were finished only after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, when the citadel held British prisoners of war.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a> the fortress was captured on 20 August 1792 by the Prussians, who opened the path to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> but abandoned Verdun after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valmy">Battle of Valmy</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> it was the last French fortress to surrender, in 1870. A new system followed, a ring of 22 polygonal forts up to 8 kilometres from the city and an inner ring of 6.</p><p>The battle of 1916 began on 21 February with a bombardment by over 1,000 German guns commanded by Erich von Falkenhayn. The Germans took Douaumont on 25 February; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a> then organised the defence and kept open the supply road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bar-le-duc">Bar-le-Duc</a>, which became known as the Voie Sacrée. Fort Vaux fell on 7 June, and the Anglo-French offensive on the Somme from 1 July drew the Germans away. Estimates put the dead at 143,000 Germans and 162,440 French. French counter-attacks under Robert Nivelle regained the forts by the end of the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Châtel Gate is the only surviving part of the medieval walls, and the 17th-century Citadel is still in military hands, though its tunnels can be visited. Verdun Cathedral was consecrated in 1147, and the Episcopal Palace was built in the 18th century by Robert de Cotte. On the battlefield stand French and German cemeteries; the largest is the French National Cemetery and Douaumont Ossuary, which holds roughly 130,000 unidentified remains. In 1916 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> awarded the city the Military Cross.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Verdun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bishops of Metz, Toul and <b>Verdun</b> and the counts of Bar ruled substantial territories around it, and Nancy became the usual ducal residence from the twelfth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the First World War the Anglo-French victory at the Battle of the Marne in September 1914 defeated Germany's plan for a quick win, and the war became one of trench warfare and attrition, reaching its height at <b>Verdun</b> and the Somme; 1.5 million French soldiers died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After a massive bombardment, the German army attacked the French fortress city of <b>Verdun</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-21">21 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1552 Henry II of France seized Metz, Toul and <b>Verdun</b>, and the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 confirmed French sovereignty over the Three Bishoprics.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-metz">Prince-Bishopric of Metz</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1552 Henry II of France occupied Toul, Metz and <b>Verdun</b> as 'protector' during his war with Charles V. French control of the Three Bishoprics was confirmed by the Peace of Westphalia in 1648, and Toul became part of the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-toul">Prince-Bishopric of Toul</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Verdun</b>, where the Carolingian empire was divided in 843, was an ancient bishopric.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-verdun">Prince-Bishopric of Verdun</a></li></ul></section>
